# d4-04 — closing set on workflow integration and solution design
human-in-the-loop|4.4|3|A team is designing a workflow and must decide whether the human checkpoint reviews every item or only the ones that meet certain conditions|Define conditions tied to what an error would cost, and review those items fully|Review everything, since conditions may not capture every case where an error would matter and full coverage is the only way to be certain nothing important is missed|Review a fixed proportion of the items that come through|Review whatever the reviewer has time for on any given day, adjusting the proportion up or down|Conditional review tied to consequence concentrates the available attention where errors cost most, which is what makes limited review capacity effective.|Reviewing everything is the unsustainable pattern this question is about. A fixed proportion ignores stakes, and capacity-driven review lets workload set the control.
process-mapping|4.1|3|A team's process has one step that fails often and one step that fails rarely but catastrophically|Weigh frequency against consequence rather than addressing whichever fails more often|Address the frequently failing step first, since it disrupts the process regularly and fixing it will produce the most visible improvement to how the process runs day to day|Address the catastrophic step first because of its severity|Address both steps at the same time so that neither of the two problems is left outstanding|Risk is frequency times consequence, and neither dimension alone determines priority. Weighing both is what produces a defensible ordering.|Prioritising by frequency ignores severity. Prioritising by severity ignores exposure, and doing both at once avoids the prioritisation the question is testing.
automation-boundaries|4.4|3|An associate is asked whether a step should be automated, and the step currently takes forty minutes a day across the team|Ask what the step's errors would cost before deciding on the time saving alone|Automate it, since forty minutes a day across a team is over three working weeks a year and that is a substantial saving to leave on the table indefinitely|Automate it if the automation can be built quickly enough|Leave it manual, since forty minutes spread across a team is a small amount per person and does not justify the effort of building and maintaining an automation|Automation decisions weigh the saving against what automated errors would cost. The time figure is only half of the input.|Deciding on the time saving alone in either direction omits the risk side. Build speed is a cost consideration rather than a decision criterion.
failure-modes|4.4|3|A workflow's most likely failure is that the model produces plausible output from an input it did not fully receive|Check that the input arrived complete before the output is produced or used|Add a review step that reads the output carefully to identify anything that looks as though it might have been produced from incomplete information during the run|Choose a stronger model so the problem occurs less often|Ask Claude to state whether it received the complete input|Truncated input producing plausible output is only caught by verifying the input, since the output gives no signal. That check has to be upstream.|Reviewing output cannot detect what was never there. A stronger model produces more plausible output from partial input, and self-reported completeness is unreliable.
repeatability|4.3|3|A team wants their workflow to produce the same result if it is run again in six months with the same inputs|Pin the configuration and record it, since defaults and models change over time|Document the workflow thoroughly so that it can be reconstructed in six months if anyone needs to reproduce the result that was originally obtained from it|Save the output somewhere so it can be consulted later|Run the workflow again now and confirm that it produces the same result|Reproducibility over time requires pinning what varies and recording it. Models and defaults change, so an unpinned workflow drifts.|Documentation describes without pinning. Saving output preserves a result rather than the ability to reproduce it, and a repeat run today says nothing about six months out.
tool-integration|4.4|3|A team's workflow reads from a system whose owners are planning a change to its data format next quarter|Agree the change with them and adapt before it lands, rather than after|Wait until the change happens and fix the workflow at that point|Build the workflow so that it accepts both of the formats|Ask the system owners to delay their change until the team has had time to prepare the workflow for the new format they intend to introduce|A known upcoming change is something to coordinate around. Adapting before it lands avoids an outage that is entirely predictable.|Waiting guarantees a break. Accepting both formats adds permanent complexity for a transition, and asking others to delay is not within the team's control.
measuring-value|4.2|3|A team wants to demonstrate their workflow's value but the benefit is that a rare expensive mistake no longer happens|Express the value as the expected cost avoided, with the frequency and cost stated|Report that the mistake has not occurred since the workflow was introduced|Report the time saved by the workflow instead of the value|Wait until enough time has passed to show a statistically meaningful reduction in the rate at which the expensive mistake occurs across the organisation|Prevention value is expressed as expected cost avoided, which makes both the frequency and the consequence explicit and lets a reader judge the estimate.|Absence over a short period is weak evidence for a rare event. Time saved measures something else, and waiting for statistical significance on a rare event takes years.
team-adoption|4.5|3|A team's workflow requires people to trust it with work they consider high-stakes, and adoption has stalled at the low-stakes cases|Treat the low-stakes adoption as the evidence base for extending upward|Encourage people to try it on higher-stakes work so that they can see for themselves that it performs just as well there as it does on the simpler cases|Mandate its use on the high-stakes work as well as the rest|Accept the current split, since using it on low-stakes work|Accumulated low-stakes performance is exactly the evidence that justifies extending scope. Using it deliberately turns caution into a staged rollout.|Encouragement without evidence asks for trust. Mandating removes the judgement, and accepting the split forgoes the value where it is largest.
handoffs|4.4|3|A workflow's two steps are owned by different people who each optimise their own step, and the overall result has got worse|Measure and optimise the end-to-end result rather than each step separately|Ask both owners to coordinate their changes so that improvements to one step do not create problems for the other step in the sequence|Combine both of the steps under a single named owner|Add a third step that corrects whatever problems arise from the interaction between the first two steps in the workflow|Local optimisation against local measures degrades the whole. Measuring end to end is what aligns the incentives with the outcome.|Coordination without a shared measure leaves both optimising locally. Single ownership is a reorganisation, and a corrective step treats the symptom.
process-mapping|4.1|3|An associate discovers that the process they are mapping is performed differently by each of the five people who do it|Establish which variations are deliberate before standardising anything|Standardise on the approach used by the most experienced person|Standardise on whichever of the variations is the fastest|Let each person keep their own variation and design the workflow to accommodate all five of the different approaches that are currently in use across the team|Variation often encodes real differences in the cases each person handles. Distinguishing deliberate adaptation from drift is what makes standardisation safe.|Standardising on seniority or speed may discard necessary adaptations. Accommodating all five builds complexity around variation nobody has understood.
human-in-the-loop|4.4|3|A team's workflow asks a person to approve output they did not request and have no context for|Give the approver the context, or move the approval to someone who has it|Ask the approver to request context from the person who initiated the work|Add a summary of the context to each item under review|Ask the approver to approve on the basis of whether the output looks internally consistent and well-formed|An approval without context is a formality. Supplying context or relocating the approval is what makes it a real decision.|On-demand context requests add delay and depend on knowing what is missing. A generated summary is not independent, and approving on form alone concedes the point.
automation-boundaries|4.4|3|A team wants to automate a step, and doing so would mean nobody in the team understands how that part of the work is done any more|Keep enough understanding in the team to supervise and change the step later|Automate it, since the point of automation is that people no longer need to concern themselves with the details of how the work is performed on a routine basis|Document the step in detail before automating any of it|Rotate people through the manual version occasionally so that the knowledge does not disappear entirely from the team over the longer term|Automating away all understanding leaves a step nobody can supervise, debug or modify. Retaining working knowledge is what keeps the team in control of it.|Treating opacity as the goal is the misconception. Documentation and rotation are both mechanisms for retaining understanding rather than alternatives to it.
failure-modes|4.4|3|A workflow retries automatically when a step fails, and a persistent failure now produces thousands of retries|Cap the retries and escalate when the cap is reached|Increase the interval between retries so that the volume of attempts is lower|Remove the retry logic so the workflow stops after one attempt|Ask someone to monitor the workflow so that persistent failures are noticed and the process can be stopped manually when this situation arises again|Retries handle transient failures and must not run unbounded against a persistent one. A cap plus escalation distinguishes the two cases.|Longer intervals reduce the rate of a still-unbounded loop. Removing retries loses transient recovery, and manual monitoring is a person doing what a cap does automatically.
repeatability|4.3|3|A team's workflow relies on a document that a colleague updates informally whenever they think of it|Establish a defined update cadence and owner for the document|Ask the colleague to notify the team whenever they update the document|Take a copy of the document for reference|Add a note to the workflow saying that the document may not be current|An input that changes informally makes the workflow's behaviour unpredictable. Defining ownership and cadence is what makes the dependency reliable.|Notification depends on remembering. A copy goes stale immediately, and a caveat documents the problem without fixing it.
tool-integration|4.4|3|An associate's integration works but sends credentials in a way the security team has not reviewed|Have the security team review it before the integration goes into use|Proceed, since the integration is internal and the credentials do not leave the organisation's own systems at any point during its normal operation|Ask the security team to review it after launch|Use a different credential with fewer permissions so that the risk is lower even|Credential handling is a security review matter and the review belongs before use. That is a governance boundary rather than a technical preference.|Internal scope does not exempt credential handling from review. Post-launch review accepts the exposure, and reducing permissions without review still bypasses the control.
measuring-value|4.2|3|A team's workflow shows a clear benefit in the pilot metrics but the people using it say it makes their work harder|Investigate the discrepancy rather than choosing which signal to believe|Trust the metrics, since they measure the outcome objectively|Trust the user reports rather than the measured pilot figures|Continue the workflow and monitor whether the user reports change over the coming months as people become more familiar with how it operates|A gap between measured benefit and experienced burden usually means the metric misses a real cost. Investigating is what reconciles them.|Choosing either signal discards information. Waiting for familiarity assumes the complaint is an adjustment problem rather than a finding.
team-adoption|4.5|3|A team's workflow was designed by someone who has since left, and the current team does not know why it works the way it does|Reconstruct the reasoning before changing anything|Change whatever seems suboptimal, since the current team are the ones who use it and are best placed to judge what would work better for them now|Leave the workflow unchanged rather than risking a regression|Rebuild the workflow from scratch so that the current team fully understands every decision that has gone into the version they are working with|Undocumented design decisions often encode constraints. Reconstructing the reasoning is what makes change safe rather than a gamble.|Changing what looks suboptimal risks removing something load-bearing. Freezing it forever is a permanent cost, and rebuilding discards a working system.
handoffs|4.4|3|A workflow passes output to a system that silently truncates anything over a certain length|Enforce the length limit at the handoff so truncation cannot happen silently|Ask Claude to produce shorter output so that the material it generates stays within the limit that the receiving system is able to accept|Increase the receiving system's length limit so nothing is cut|Check the receiving system periodically to identify records that appear to have been truncated so they can be corrected when the problem is found|Silent truncation at a boundary is a contract violation that must be caught where it occurs. Enforcing the limit makes the failure explicit.|Shorter output reduces the incidence without preventing it. Raising the limit may not be possible, and periodic checks find truncation after the data is already wrong.
process-mapping|4.1|3|A team wants to measure a process before changing it, but measuring would itself take three weeks of effort|Measure the one or two things the decision actually depends on|Measure the process thoroughly, since a change made without a baseline cannot be evaluated afterwards and the three weeks is an investment in knowing whether it worked|Skip the measurement and change the process|Measure after the change instead, since that will show how the new process performs and the old one is being replaced anyway so its performance matters less|Measurement should be scoped to what the decision needs rather than to the process as a whole. That is what makes a baseline affordable.|Full measurement over-invests. Skipping it loses the baseline entirely, and measuring only afterwards leaves nothing to compare against.
automation-boundaries|4.4|3|A team is considering automating a step where the input occasionally contains something the model should refuse to process|Design the refusal path deliberately rather than leaving it to the model's judgement|Automate it and rely on the model to decline when it encounters material it should not process|Keep the step manual so that the incidental knowledge is retained|Automate it and add a review step that identifies any cases where the model processed something it should have declined to handle in the first place|A refusal path that matters should be designed and tested rather than assumed. Defining what the workflow does with such input is part of the design.|Relying on default behaviour leaves the boundary undefined and untested. Manual handling forgoes the automation, and after-the-fact review catches failures rather than preventing them.
failure-modes|4.4|3|A team's workflow was designed around one failure mode and a different one has now occurred twice|Revisit the failure analysis, since the assumptions behind it have been shown incomplete|Add handling for the new failure mode alongside the existing handling|Increase the level of monitoring applied|Treat the two occurrences as unusual and continue with the current design|Two occurrences of an unanticipated mode is evidence that the original analysis was incomplete. Revisiting it is what surfaces what else was missed.|Patching the new mode addresses this one and leaves the analysis unrevised. More monitoring watches without understanding, and dismissing two occurrences ignores the evidence.
repeatability|4.3|3|A team's workflow gives consistent results within a single session and different results across sessions|Identify what differs between sessions, since something is varying that should not be|Accept the variation, since some difference between sessions is a normal property of working with a model and the results within any single session remain consistent|Run everything within a single session to avoid the variation entirely|Average the results across several sessions so that the variation between them is smoothed out in the figure the team actually reports and uses|Consistency within sessions and variation across them points at a session-scoped input such as accumulated context or configuration. That is findable.|Accepting it abandons a diagnosable problem. Forcing single sessions is a workaround, and averaging conceals rather than explains the variation.
tool-integration|4.4|3|An associate wants to integrate Claude into a process, and the integration would be the only automated part of an otherwise manual workflow|Check whether the manual steps around it would absorb the benefit|Build the integration, since automating any part of a manual process delivers a saving and the surrounding manual steps do not prevent that part from being faster|Automate the whole of the process rather than only this single step|Automate the step and measure whether the overall process time falls|A single automated step inside a manual process often produces work that then waits. Checking whether the surrounding steps absorb the gain is the prior question.|Assuming the local saving reaches the outcome is the error. Full automation may not be feasible, and measuring afterwards spends the effort before asking.
measuring-value|4.2|3|A team's workflow reduces errors but the errors it prevents were previously caught by a downstream check that still runs|Check whether the downstream check can now be reduced, since otherwise the saving is not realised|Report the error reduction, since fewer errors being produced is a genuine improvement|Remove the downstream check entirely now that errors are prevented upstream|Continue running both, since redundant checks provide additional assurance and the cost of the downstream check was already being incurred before the workflow was introduced|If a downstream check still absorbs the same cost, preventing errors upstream has not yet produced a saving. Whether the check can be reduced is what realises it.|Reporting the reduction claims a benefit that has not landed. Removing the check outright is premature, and keeping both indefinitely means the workflow pays for itself twice.
team-adoption|4.5|3|A team introduces a workflow and the people who use it most are the ones who were already most productive|Check whether the workflow is widening rather than closing the gap|Consider the rollout a success, since the workflow is being adopted and the people using it are getting value from it in their day-to-day work|Target the least productive users in the next phase of the rollout|Accept the pattern, since more capable users adopting new tools more readily is a common and expected outcome that does not require any particular intervention|If a tool amplifies existing capability, the aggregate improves while the spread widens. Noticing that is what tells you whether the rollout met its goal.|Adoption by the already-productive is not the same as the intended outcome. Targeting others assumes the cause, and accepting the pattern skips the check.
handoffs|4.4|3|A workflow's output includes a confidence indicator that the receiving person has learned to ignore because it is rarely informative|Remove it or make it meaningful, since an ignored signal is worse than none|Explain to the receiving person what the confidence indicator means|Replace it with a numeric score|Keep it, since it costs nothing to produce and may occasionally be useful|A signal people have learned to ignore trains them to ignore signals generally. Either making it informative or removing it is the honest response.|Explaining an uninformative signal does not make it informative. A numeric version of the same weak signal is no better, and keeping it normalises ignoring indicators.
process-mapping|4.1|3|A team is asked to make a process faster, and the process is already faster than the downstream team can consume its output|Look downstream, since speeding up production ahead of consumption creates a queue|Speed up the process as requested, since that is what has been asked for and the downstream team's capacity is not within this team's control or responsibility|Slow the process down so it matches what the downstream team can take|Speed up the process and store the additional output until the downstream team is ready to consume it|Producing faster than the next stage can absorb builds inventory rather than delivering value. The constraint is downstream.|Delivering the request as stated produces a growing queue. Slowing down is not the ask, and buffering output is inventory rather than throughput.
automation-boundaries|4.4|3|An associate is asked to automate a decision that is currently made by consensus between three people|Establish whether the consensus encodes a rule or genuine disagreement|Automate it using the position the three people usually converge on, since that represents the collective judgement they would have reached anyway in most cases|Keep the decision manual rather than automating any part of the step|Automate it and route to the three people only in the cases where the model is uncertain about which way the decision should go on the evidence available|Consensus may be an agreed rule applied by three people or a genuine negotiation. Which it is determines whether the decision can be specified at all.|Encoding the usual outcome assumes a rule exists. Keeping it manual forgoes the analysis, and uncertainty routing presumes specifiability.
failure-modes|4.4|3|A workflow has good error handling for the failures the team anticipated and no handling for anything else|Add a defined behaviour for unanticipated failures rather than leaving them undefined|Extend the error handling to cover additional failure modes as the team identifies them through experience of running the workflow over time|Choose a stronger model so that the truncation problem happens less|Accept the current coverage, since the anticipated failures are the ones most likely to occur and handling every conceivable failure is not a realistic goal|Unanticipated failures need a defined default such as stopping safely and escalating. That is what stops an unknown failure from becoming an undefined one.|Extending coverage case by case leaves each new mode undefined until it happens. A stronger model does not define behaviour, and accepting the gap leaves the workflow open-ended.
repeatability|4.3|3|A team's workflow is run by four people and produces four noticeably different levels of quality|Standardise the parts that vary rather than training four people toward one standard|Train all four people to the standard achieved by whoever currently produces the best results|Have the best performing person run everything for the team|Accept the variation as the normal range of quality that different people produce when performing the same task in their own individual ways|Person-dependent quality means the workflow leaves too much to the operator. Moving the variable parts into fixed configuration is what makes quality independent of who runs it.|Training toward a standard leaves the dependency in place. Single-operator running is a bottleneck, and accepting variation forfeits repeatability.
tool-integration|4.4|3|A team's integration works reliably but nobody has recorded what it does, and the person who built it has moved teams|Document what it does and who owns it now, before anything needs changing|Leave it alone, since it works reliably and there is no immediate need to spend time documenting something that is not currently causing any problems for anyone|Rebuild it so the current team understands it|Contact the person who built it whenever a question arises about how the integration works or what it is doing in a particular situation|An undocumented, unowned integration is a problem the moment it needs changing or breaks. Documenting and assigning ownership now is cheap.|Leaving it works until it does not. Rebuilding discards a working system, and depending on someone in another team is a fragile arrangement.
measuring-value|4.2|3|A team's workflow is measured on how many items it processes, and the team has started routing easy items to it to improve the number|Change the measure, since it is driving behaviour that does not serve the goal|Ask the team to stop selecting easy items so that the throughput figure reflects the workflow's performance across a representative mix of the work|Keep the measure and add a quality measure alongside it|Accept the selection, since processing easy items through the workflow is still productive work|A measure that can be gamed will be. Changing it so it aligns with the goal is what removes the incentive rather than policing it.|Asking people not to respond to an incentive rarely works. A second measure alongside a gameable one leaves the incentive, and accepting the selection accepts a meaningless figure.
team-adoption|4.5|3|A team's workflow requires a small amount of setup per user, and half the team have not completed it|Do the setup for them, since a small barrier is stopping half the benefit|Send instructions explaining how to complete the setup so that the remaining team members can get themselves up and running when they have a moment|Remind people weekly until the setup is complete|Accept the partial adoption, since the people who completed the setup are presumably the ones who found the workflow most useful for their own work|Small barriers stop adoption disproportionately. Removing the barrier entirely is cheaper than repeatedly asking people to cross it.|Instructions and reminders both leave the barrier in place. Inferring preference from an uncrossed barrier confuses friction with disinterest.
handoffs|4.4|3|A workflow's steps are each fast, but work waits between them because each step runs on a different schedule|Align the schedules or make the steps trigger each other|Speed up the individual steps so that the total processing time falls|Combine all of the steps together into one single larger step|Run each step more frequently so that work waits a shorter time between the completion of one step and the start of the next one in the sequence|Elapsed time is dominated by waiting between steps rather than by the steps themselves. Triggering removes the wait rather than shortening it.|Faster steps do not touch the waits. Combining steps is a larger change than needed, and more frequent runs reduce the wait without eliminating it.
process-mapping|4.1|3|A team maps their process and finds that half the steps produce output nobody downstream actually uses|Stop producing the unused output|Automate the production of the unused output with Claude|Ask downstream teams whether they might use it|Keep producing it, since somebody may have needed it at some point and stopping could cause a problem that is not currently visible to the team|Work producing unused output is waste. Stopping it is a larger and simpler gain than making it cheaper to produce.|Automating waste entrenches it. Canvassing for hypothetical users looks for a reason to continue, and preserving it against an unknown need pays permanently.
automation-boundaries|4.4|3|A team can automate a step, and the person who currently performs it uses the time to notice patterns that inform other decisions|Identify what that noticing produces and preserve it deliberately|Automate the step, since noticing patterns is not the purpose of the step and the person will find other opportunities to observe the same information elsewhere|Leave the step manual and recruit a replacement person to be trained|Automate the step and ask the person to review the automated output periodically|Manual work often produces incidental value nobody designed. Identifying it before automating is what stops a hidden benefit from disappearing silently.|Assuming the noticing will happen elsewhere is unfounded. Leaving it manual is more than needed, and periodic review of output is not the same as working through the material.
failure-modes|4.4|3|A workflow fails in a way that is obvious to the team but invisible to the customers who receive its output|Add detection at the point the customer would experience the problem|Ask the team to check the output more carefully so that the failures they can recognise are caught before anything reaches the customers who would not notice them|Notify the customers whenever one of these failures actually occurs|Accept the situation, since the team notices the failures and can correct them when they are identified during the course of their normal work|A failure invisible to the person affected needs detection oriented to their experience rather than to the team's. That is where the control belongs.|More careful team checking still catches only what the team recognises. Notification follows detection, and relying on incidental team awareness leaves customers exposed.
repeatability|4.3|3|A team wants their workflow to be usable by a different team with different data but the same underlying task|Separate the parts that are specific to this team's data from the general method|Give the other team a copy of the workflow and let them adjust whatever does not fit their own data and processes|Rebuild the workflow from scratch for the other team involved|Document the workflow in detail so the other team can understand how it works and decide for themselves what they need to change about it|Reusability comes from separating the general method from the local specifics. That is what makes a second deployment a configuration exercise rather than a rebuild.|Handing over a copy to adapt distributes the same untangling work. Rebuilding duplicates effort, and documentation describes without separating.
measuring-value|4.2|3|A team is asked for the return on their workflow and the largest benefit is that a task can now be done at all|Describe the new capability rather than forcing it into a time-saved figure|Estimate what the task would have cost to do manually and report that figure as the saving the workflow has delivered to the organisation|Report the cost figure without any benefit figure alongside|Report the time saved on the other smaller tasks the workflow also performs|A capability that did not previously exist is not a saving, and describing it as one distorts it. Naming it as new capability is the honest account.|A hypothetical manual cost invents a baseline that never existed. Reporting cost alone omits the benefit, and substituting a minor measurable benefit understates the main one.
team-adoption|4.5|3|A team's workflow works well and a colleague in another team wants to copy it for a task that is superficially similar|Check whether the underlying task is actually the same before copying it across|Share the workflow, since it works well and the colleague can adapt whatever does not fit their own situation|Advise the colleague to build their own version from scratch instead|Share the workflow and offer to help them set it up so that the transfer goes smoothly and they get the benefit as quickly as possible in their own team|Superficial similarity often hides different requirements. Checking whether the tasks match is what determines whether copying will work at all.|Sharing on surface similarity risks a poor fit discovered later. Advising a rebuild forgoes reuse, and helping with setup accelerates a transfer that may be wrong.
